WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 68: The Tuskegee Airmen: The Red Tails of the 332nd Fighter Group

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:16.400
[Rebecca] In the dark days before American entry into World War II, a deeply segregated nation operated on a military doctrine rooted in prejudice.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:29.200
[Rebecca] A 1925 Army War College report had falsely claimed that African Americans lacked the intelligence, technical aptitude, and courage to operate high performance military aircraft in combat.

5
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:41.520
[Rebecca] In 1941, under pressure from civil rights leaders and First Lady Alina Roosevelt, the War Department initiated what was called an experiment at Tuskegee Army Air Field in the Alabama Black Belt.

6
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:50.640
[Rebecca] What emerged from that Red Alabama clay was one of the most elite, respected, and lethal fighter groups in military history, the Red Tails.

7
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:57.520
[Rebecca] George brings us the soaring, courageous saga of the Tuskegee Airmen.

8
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:08.400
[George] In the 1930s, the United States Army Air Corps was a strictly segregated institution that refused to train African American pilots, mechanics, or navigators.

9
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:23.120
[George] Military leaders justified this discrimination by citing a pseudoscientific 1925 Army War College study that claimed black men were inherently incapable of mastering modern mechanical instruments or demonstrating courage under fire.

10
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:35.040
[George] But civil rights organizations, led by the NAACP, the Black Press, and Howard University student activists, mounted a relentless political campaign for equal opportunity.

11
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.800
[George] In 1940, with war clouds gathering over Europe and Asia, President Franklin D.

12
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.600
[George] Roosevelt announced the formation of the 99th Pursuit Squadron, the first all black flying unit.

13
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:59.600
[George] To train the pilots, the war department selected Tuskegee, Alabama, an isolated town in the rural black belt anchored by the prestigious Tuskegee Institute, founded decades earlier by Booker T.

14
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.000
[George] Washington.

15
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:13.480
[George] Military planners widely expected the Tuskegee experiment to fail, believing that black recruits would wash out of rigorous flight training, thus validating the armed forces' segregationist policies.

16
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:24.440
[George] Black college students and community leaders marched outside the war department in Washington, demanding that African Americans be granted the right to serve their country as combat aviators.

17
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:36.280
[George] The dramatic events unfolded against a backdrop of deep cultural transformation, as rural communities confronted the rapid onset of modern industrial technology and national media attention.

18
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:50.760
[George] African American aviators faced systemic racism and widespread prejudice within the military establishment, where senior officers falsely claimed that black men lacked the psychological fortitude and mechanical aptitude for aerial combat.

19
00:02:50.760 --> 00:03:00.280
[George] The turning point for the program arrived in March 1941, when First Lady Alina Roosevelt visited the civilian pilot training airfield at Tuskegee.

20
00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:00.680
[George] Mrs.

21
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.200
[George] Roosevelt met C.

22
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:12.920
[George] Alfred Chief Anderson, a pioneering African American aviator who had taught himself to fly in the 1920s and served as the chief civilian flight instructor.

23
00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:23.160
[George] Despite frantic objections from her secret service detail, the First Lady walked out onto the tarmac and asked Anderson, Can black people really fly airplanes?

24
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.320
[George] Anderson smiled warmly and replied, certainly we can, ma'am.

25
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.160
[George] Would you like to go up with me?

26
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:29.880
[George] Mrs.

27
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:40.520
[George] Roosevelt stepped into the cockpit of a single engine Piper J 3 Cub, and Anderson flew the first lady on a 40-minute scenic flight over the green Alabama countryside.

28
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.520
[George] Upon landing safely, Mrs.

29
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.600
[George] Roosevelt declared to journalists, Well, you can fly all right.

30
00:03:47.600 --> 00:04:01.520
[George] The photograph of Alina Roosevelt smiling beside a black pilot made front page news worldwide, securing vital federal funding for the construction of Motten Field and ensuring that the Tuskegee military program would move forward.

31
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:13.440
[George] Elina Roosevelt flight with Chief Anderson shattered decades of racist military propaganda, demonstrating to the entire nation that skill and courage in the air knew no racial boundary.

32
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:24.400
[George] Eyewitness accounts recorded in local diaries and regional newspapers captured the tension and uncertainty of the moment, preserving vivid details for future generations of historians.

33
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:37.440
[George] Chief Anderson was a master aviator who had earned his commercial pilot's license against immense obstacles, inspiring generations of young black trainees with his flawless stick and rudder technique and patient instruction.

34
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:45.280
[George] Training at Motten Field was intentionally engineered to be the most demanding and rigorous flight curriculum in the United States military.

35
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:54.800
[George] Because white commanders were searching for any excuse to shutter the program, the standard for Tuskegee cadets was set significantly higher than at white flight schools.

36
00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:01.120
[George] Only the top echelon of college graduates, track athletes, and mathematics scholars were accepted.

37
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:16.400
[George] Cadets woke at dawn to the scorching Alabama humidity, enduring grueling physical calisthenics, advanced aeronautical engineering classes, and relentless crosswind landing drills in PT 17 steermen biplanes and at six Texans.

38
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:23.840
[George] In March 1942, the first class of five cadets earned their Silver Army wings, led by Captain Benjamin O.

39
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:33.320
[George] Davis J.R., a brilliant West Point graduate who had endured four years of silent ostracism at the military academy without a single white cadet speaking to him.

40
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:44.680
[George] Davis assumed command of the 99th Fighter Squadron, instilling in his men an ironclad ethos of military precision, spotless discipline, and technical perfection.

41
00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:57.800
[George] Instructors at Motton Fields subjected cadets to grueling aerodynamic examinations, demanding absolute perfection in instrument flying, navigation, and aerobatic precision under the scorching Alabama Sun.

42
00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:58.680
[George] Benjamin O.

43
00:05:58.680 --> 00:05:59.880
[George] Davis J.R.

44
00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:12.680
[George] was a graduate of West Point who had endured four years of silent ostracism from white cadets, emerging with an ironclad sense of military discipline and leadership that commanded absolute respect from his men.

45
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:20.920
[George] In April 1943, the 99th Fighter Squadron shipped out to North Africa, entering combat over the Mediterranean.

46
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:35.560
[George] Flying Curtis P-40 Warhawks, the Tuskegee pilots supported Allied landings in Sicily and Italy, executing dive-bombing runs against German artillery emplacements and engaging in vicious dogfights with Luftwaffe Messerschmitt's.

47
00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:45.480
[George] Despite their success, racist commanders in Washington attempted to have the squadron removed from combat, falsely alleging that their combat record was subpar.

48
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:59.160
[George] In response, Colonel Davis flew back to the Pentagon and presented meticulous statistical evidence demonstrating that his pilots achieved mission success rates equal to or exceeding every white squadron in the Mediterranean theater.

49
00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.960
[George] The War Department was forced to back down.

50
00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:23.760
[George] In 1944, the 99th was joined by three additional Tuskegee squadrons, the 100th, 301st, and 302nd, to form the 332nd Fighter Group, commanded by Davis, and transitioning to state-of-the-art Republic P-47 Thunderbolts and North American P-51 Mustangs.

51
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:35.440
[George] The 99th Fighter Squadron earned respect through relentless combat discipline, flying low-level ground strafing missions through intense German flak over the beaches of Anzio and Salerno.

52
00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:49.760
[George] The distinctive crimson paint on the tails of their P-51 Mustangs became an unmistakable badge of honor in the skies over Germany, instilling confidence in Allied bomber crews who cheered when the Red Tails swept into defensive position.

53
00:07:49.760 --> 00:08:02.000
[George] To distinguish their aircraft from other fighter groups in the crowded European skies, Colonel Davis ordered the empennage of every P-51 Mustang in the group to be painted a distinctive, brilliant crimson.

54
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.640
[George] The legend of the Red Tails was born.

55
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:22.640
[George] Operating from Ramatelli Airfield in southern Italy, the Red Tails were assigned to the 15th Air Force, tasked with flying long-range escort missions for heavy B-17 Flying Fortress and B-24 Liberator bombers deep into the heart of Nazi Germany, Austria, and Romania.

56
00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:33.520
[George] While other fighter escort groups frequently abandoned their bombers to chase German fighters across the horizon to boost their personal ace scores, Colonel Davis issued an iron directive.

57
00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:41.680
[George] The Red Tails were to stay tightly glued to the bomber formations, prioritizing the survival of the American crews above all else.

58
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:52.080
[George] White bomber pilots, looking out through their plexiglass turrets, breathed sighs of immense relief whenever the sleek mustangs with the red tails pulled up alongside their wings.

59
00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] The bomber crews christened them the Red Tail Angels, marveling at their fierce, selfless defense against swarms of German interceptors.

60
00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:13.000
[George] Bomber crews of the 15th Air Force began specifically requesting the Red Tails for their dangerous missions over the oil fields of Plosti, knowing that the red-tailed Mustangs would never abandon their charges.

61
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:25.880
[George] White bomber crews who had once doubted black aviators openly requested the Red Tails as their preferred escort, forming a deep combat bond that transcended racial barriers in the high, freezing skies above Europe.

62
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:32.040
[George] The Tuskegee Airmen compiled one of the most distinguished combat records in modern military history.

63
00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:43.240
[George] Over the course of the war, they flew 15,533 combat sorties across Europe and the Mediterranean, destroying 261 enemy aircraft.

64
00:09:43.240 --> 00:10:02.680
[George] In March 1945, during an epic 1600-mile round trip mission escorting bombers to the heavily defended Daimlerbentz tank assembly factories in Berlin, the Red Tails encountered Germany's revolutionary Messerschmitt Me 262 twin-engine jet fighters, the fastest aircraft on Earth.

65
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.920
[George] Flying 100 miles an hour faster than any propeller fighter.

66
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:19.640
[George] In a fierce dogfight over Berlin, three Tuskegee pilots, Roscoe Brown, Charles Brantley and Earl Lane, outmaneuvered the advanced German jets and shot three of them out of the sky in a single afternoon.

67
00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:35.800
[George] For over half a century, it was believed the Red Tails never lost a single bomber under their escort, while modern military records revealed that 25 bombers were shot down across hundreds of missions, their loss rate was less than half the average of other escort groups.

68
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:41.000
[George] Earning the 332nd Three Distinguished Presidential Unit citations.

69
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:51.840
[George] The victory over the German jet fighters over Berlin proved that the Tuskegee Aviators were among the most skilled, daring, and lethal fighter pilots in the entire Allied coalition.

70
00:10:51.840 --> 00:11:03.920
[George] The courage and resilience demonstrated by everyday citizens during this critical trial inspired observers across the country, showing that ordinary people can rise to meet extraordinary challenges.

71
00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:18.160
[George] The extraordinary combat record of the Tuskegee Airmen directly dismantled the segregationist arguments of military brass, demonstrating that courage, technical excellence, and patriotism were entirely independent of skin color.

72
00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:29.360
[George] The Tuskegee Airmen returned home to an America that was still segregated by law, yet their heroic sacrifice dealt a fatal blow to the institutional foundations of white supremacy.

73
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.320
[George] In 1948, President Harry S.

74
00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:44.000
[George] Truman signed Executive Order 9981, officially desegregating the United States Armed Forces, the first major federal institution to achieve racial integration.

75
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:44.880
[George] Benjamin O.

76
00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.000
[George] Davis J.R.

77
00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.840
[George] rose to become the first African American four-star general in the United States Air Force.

78
00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:55.040
[George] In 2007, President George W.

79
00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:06.880
[George] Bush presented the Congressional Gold Medal, the nation's highest civilian honor, to 300 surviving Tuskegee Airmen in the Capitol Rotunda, standing proudly in their crimson blazers.

80
00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:22.320
[George] These veteran heroes were celebrated not only as premier combat aviators who defeated Nazi tyranny in the skies of Europe, but as trailblazers of civil rights whose courage, patriotism, and excellence transformed the moral landscape of the United States.

81
00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:33.640
[George] The Congressional Gold Medal ceremony in the Capitol Rotunda honored 300 elderly veterans whose patriotism and excellence helped defeat fascism abroad and dismantle segregation at held.

82
00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:45.880
[George] Today, historic markers and museum collections honor these legendary figures, reminding us that the spirit of American adventure and perseverance continues to guide our shared journey forward.

83
00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:58.760
[George] The legacy of the Tuskegee Airmen remains an enduring testament to the power of perseverance and excellence, inspiring countless young Americans to pursue careers in aviation, science, and public service.

84
00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:24.280
[Rebecca] Join us next week for one of the strangest cosmic events in history the day an Alabama woman was struck by a media.

